tans, with defined dosing regimens (e.g., rizatriptan at 5–10 mg, max 30 mg/day; zolmitriptan in oral or nasal spray forms at 2.5–5 mg). Their inclusion in treatment algorithms underscores their clinical value, but also their potential to contribute to chronic headache if misused. [Data: Entities (99, 548, 549); Relationships (147, 148, 736, 737, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Triptans are effective for acute migraine but pose a risk of Medication Overuse Headache (MOH) if overused\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Ergots, particularly ergotamine, are a class of medications used for the acute treatment of migraine attacks. While effective in relieving migraine symptoms, their use carries a significant risk of Medication Overuse Headache (MOH) when taken on 10 or more days per month. This overuse threshold is consistent with other acute medications implicated in MOH, such as triptans and opioids. The relationship between ergots and chronic migraine is further reinforced by the fact that the relief of migraine attacks by ergots is part of the diagnostic criteria for chronic migraine, suggesting that their use may be both therapeutic and potentially contributory to the chronic condition. This dual role underscores the need for careful monitoring and adherence to prescribed dosing schedules to prevent the development of MOH. The inclusion of ergots in treatment pathways highlights their clinical utility but also their potential to exacerbate headache frequency if not used judiciously. [Data: Entities (100, 97, 470); Relationships (635, 208, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Ergots are effective for acute migraine but can contribute to Medication Overuse Headache (MOH) if used excessively\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is a commonly used simple analgesic for headache relief, but its overuse is a key diagnostic criterion for Medication Overuse Headache (MOH). Specifically, using acet