 of chronic migraine. [Data: Entities (97, 470, 164); Relationships (625, 109, 208, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Medication Overuse Headache (MOH) is a major cause of chronic migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Treatment strategies for migraine are often structured using stepped and stratified approaches, which guide the selection and sequencing of medications based on severity and treatment response. In a stepped treatment model, triptans are recommended if initial treatment with acetaminophen or NSAIDs is insufficient. In a stratified treatment model, triptans are specifically indicated for moderate or severe migraine attacks. These frameworks help ensure that patients receive appropriate therapy while minimizing the risk of overuse. The integration of triptans into both models highlights their role as a second-line or targeted therapy, but also emphasizes the need for careful monitoring to prevent overuse and the development of MOH. These structured approaches are essential for optimizing treatment outcomes and reducing the risk of chronic headache. [Data: Entities (99, 125); Relationships (147, 148, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Stepped and stratified treatment models guide appropriate use of triptans and other medications to prevent overuse\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The risk of Medication Overuse Headache (MOH) is not limited to prescription medications but extends to a broad range of acute headache treatments, including combination analgesics, barbiturates, and other commonly used drugs. This wide spectrum of implicated medications underscores the importance of comprehensive patient education and monitoring. The overuse of any of these medications—defined as use on 10 or more days per month—can lead to the development of MOH, regardless of the specific drug. This highlights the need for a holistic approach to headache management that considers all forms of acute treatment, includin